UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 8, Lesson 2: Respectful retelling and protocols', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Protocols are respectful rules for sharing First Nations stories and knowledge. Before using a story, ask who holds it and whether permission has been given. Credit the storyteller and the Nation, do not change the words or images, and do not copy art styles or symbols as if they were yours.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA student wants to retell a story they heard once and add a new ending. List two protocol questions they should ask first.\n\nFEEDBACK\nWho holds this story, and do I have permission to share it? How should the storyteller and Nation be credited? Changing a story is not acceptable.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBelieving that anyone can retell a story if the retelling is kind ignores who holds the story and whether permission was given.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nWrite a protocol checklist of five questions to ask before sharing a First Nations story. Then write a short story of your own about a place you care for, and explain why it was right to choose your own story.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"HSIE","learning_intention":"Protocols are respectful rules for sharing First Nations stories and knowledge. Before using a story, ask who holds it and whether permission has been given. Credit the storyteller and the Nation, do not change the words or images, and do not copy art styles or symbols as if they were yours.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Apply and respond","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["HS2-ACH-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Write a protocol checklist of five questions to ask before sharing a First Nations story. Then write a short story of your own about a place you care for, and explain why it was right to choose your own story. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Who holds this story, and do I have permission to share it? How should the storyteller and Nation be credited? Changing a story is not acceptable. Reasoning: I used protocol by attending to retell and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Believing that anyone can retell a story if the retelling is kind ignores who holds the story and whether permission was given. Correction: Who holds this story, and do I have permission to share it? How should the storyteller and Nation be credited? Changing a story is not acceptable. Why: the correction restores accurate use of protocol.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Believing that anyone can retell a story if the retelling is kind ignores who holds the story and whether permission was given. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating protocol on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with protocol, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Who holds this story, and do I have permission to share it? How should the storyteller and Nation be credited? Changing a story is not acceptable. This matches the lesson skill for protocol.","options":["Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Who holds this story, and do I have permission to share it? How should the storyteller and Nation be credited? Changing a story is not acceptable.","Believing that anyone can retell a story if the retelling is kind ignores who holds the story and whether permission was given."],"question":"A student wants to retell a story they heard once and add a new ending. List two protocol questions they should ask first. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Believing that anyone can retell a story if the retelling is kind ignores who holds the story and whether permission was given. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Careful use of retell and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","Believing that anyone can retell a story if the retelling is kind ignores who holds the story and whether permission was given.","Protocols are respectful rules for sharing First Nations stories and knowledge. Before using a story, ask who holds it and whether permission has been given.…","Who holds this story, and do I have permission to share it? How should the storyteller and Nation be credited? Changing a story is not acceptable."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising protocol?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named retell and explain why it matters, not by copying the model.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the retell and explain why it matters used."],"question":"A learner is practising protocol. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Who holds this story, and do I have permission to share it? How should the storyteller and Nation be credited? Changing a story is not acceptable. uses sources and places appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Believing that anyone can retell a story if the retelling is kind ignores who holds the story and whether permission was given.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Who holds this story, and do I have permission to share it? How should the storyteller and Nation be credited? Changing a story is not acceptable."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about protocol?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Believing that anyone can retell a story if the retelling is kind ignores who holds the story and whether permission was given.","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about protocol."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about retell and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Example: a class wants to share a story at assembly. They ask their local Aboriginal education officer, who suggests the school invite a…","Believing that anyone can retell a story if the retelling is kind ignores who holds the story and whether permission was given.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Protocols are respectful rules for sharing First Nations stories and knowledge. Before using a story, ask… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Believing that anyone can retell a story if the retelling is kind ignores who holds the story and whether permission was given.","Write a protocol checklist of five questions to ask before sharing a First Nations story. Then write a short story of your own about a place you…","Retype the worked example with no new example."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of protocol?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating protocol on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Who holds this story, and do I have permission to share it? How should the storyteller and Nation be credited? Changing a story is not acceptable. Reasoning: I used protocol by attending to retell and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with protocol, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Believing that anyone can retell a story if the retelling is kind ignores who holds the story and whether permission was given. Correction: Who holds this story, and do I have permission to share it? How should the storyteller and Nation be credited? Changing a story is not acceptable. Why: the correction restores accurate use of protocol.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-hsie-y3-w08-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA HSIE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (HS2-*); content-point mapping and local First Nations protocols remain pending human review.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise protocol.","Read the mission before you start mapping or using sources.","Keep the place, people or source you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the source or place detail that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore sources or respectful practice.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread names, places, dates and sequence.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact source detail or place feature before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key evidence.","Say what each source or place detail shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or use of sources, not only how neat the poster looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, person or source that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new sources or places."]},"subject":"First Nations Histories and Cultures","success_criteria":["I can use protocol with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify retell and explain why it matters.","I can practise credit independently on a new prompt.","I can notice permission needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Protocols are respectful rules for sharing First Nations stories and knowledge. Before using a story, ask who holds it and whether permission has been given. Credit the storyteller and the Nation, do not change the words or images, and do not copy art styles or symbols as if…","options":["A guess with no evidence.","Protocols are respectful rules for sharing First Nations stories and knowledge. Before using a story, ask who holds it and whether permission has been given. Credit the storyteller and the Nation, do not change the words or images, and do not copy art styles or symbols as if they were yours.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about protocol?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a class wants to share a story at assembly. They ask their local Aboriginal education officer, who suggests the school invite a Custodian or read the published book with its credits shown. The class writes a thank-you note and puts the credits on the program.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA student wants to retell a story they heard once and add a new ending. List two protocol questions they should ask first.\n\nFEEDBACK\nWho holds this story, and do I have permission to share it? How should the storyteller and Nation be credited? Changing a story is not acceptable.","explain":"Protocols are respectful rules for sharing First Nations stories and knowledge. Before using a story, ask who holds it and whether permission has been given. Credit the storyteller and the Nation, do not change the words or images, and do not copy art styles or symbols as if they were yours.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Believing that anyone can retell a story if the retelling is kind ignores who holds the story and whether permission was given. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Protocol questions before sharing.","caption":"Never change the words.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\