name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about protocol?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a class wants to share a story at assembly. They ask their local Aboriginal education officer, who suggests the school invite a Custodian or read the published book with its credits shown. The class writes a thank-you note and puts the credits on the program. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Choose your own story.","caption":"Share only what is yours to share.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Own story</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">a place you care for</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Not yours</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">copied art or symbols</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about protocol?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBelieving that anyone can retell a story if the retelling is kind ignores who holds the story and whether permission was given. Here is a more productive model: Example: a class wants to share a story at assembly. They ask their local Aboriginal education officer, who suggests the school invite a Custodian or read the published book with its credits shown. The class writes a thank-you note and puts the credits on the program.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nWho holds this story, and do I have permission to share it? How should the storyteller and Nation be credited? Changing a story is not acceptable.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Believing that anyone can retell a story if the retelling is kind ignores who holds the story and whether permission was given. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about protocol?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nProtocols are respectful rules for sharing First Nations stories and knowledge. Before using a story, ask who holds it and whether permission has been given. Credit the storyteller and the Nation, do not change the words or images, and do not copy art styles or symbols as if they were yours. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same HSIE idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Transfer to a new inquiry"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 8, Lesson 2: Respectful retelling and protocols","unit_number":2,"unit_title":"First Nations Peoples and Country","week":8,"week_in_unit":3,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: a class wants to share a story at assembly. They ask their local Aboriginal education officer, who suggests the school invite a Custodian or read the published book with its credits shown. The class writes a thank-you note and puts the credits on the program.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the sources, places or community details used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Protocol questions before sharing.","caption":"Never change the words.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=